# EA3-only kernel config, applied on top of board/ea-common/linux/common.fragment.
#
# The EA3 is the same computer as the EA1 (CE5310, 1.5 GB, same eMMC layout and
# bootloader), so almost everything it needs is already in the common fragment.
# Two things differ, and both simplify the build:
#
#   1. THERE IS NO WI-FI on this board. No ath9k, no /sys/class/ieee80211, no
#      wlan interface — see https://the1truejoe.github.io/openHC/ea/ea3/. The wireless stack is dropped
#      entirely, which also buys back image size against CEFDK's ~7 MB
#      bootlinux copy window.
#
#   2. REACHABILITY IS WIRED, and it is the only path. e1000 is enabled in the
#      common fragment. TESTED on hardware: 7.1.8 boots to a login prompt on
#      this board, but e1000 probe fails with a bare -EIO and there is no eth0.
#      STILL UNSOLVED. It is not a host-bridge-window problem — that theory was
#      tried, `nocrs` was removed on the strength of it, and the only result was
#      an earlier hang (see the command line at the bottom of this file).

# No radio on this board. Explicitly off rather than merely absent, so the
# inherited i386 defconfig cannot drag the wireless stack back in.
# CONFIG_WLAN is not set
# CONFIG_CFG80211 is not set
# CONFIG_MAC80211 is not set

# ── BCM53125 managed switch, via DSA ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# The EA3's two rear RJ45s are ports on a Broadcom BCM53125 sitting on SPI
# (spi0.1), not on MDIO; the e1000 MAC is the CPU port behind it. Bringing the
# switch up under DSA turns those two jacks into their own netdevs (lan1/lan2)
# alongside the master eth0, which is the point of doing this at all — the
# vendor's out-of-tree spi-bcm53125 driver exposes no such thing.
#
# THREE PIECES ARE NEEDED AND ALL THREE ARE HERE:
#   1. the SPI master        — spi-pxa2xx-pci binds PCI 8086:2e6a (CE4100 port),
#                              which is the same function the CE5310 exposes
#   2. the switch driver     — b53 with its SPI binding
#   3. board glue            — ea3/patches/linux/0003 registers the switch as an
#                              SPI device at chip-select 1 and hands DSA the
#                              port map, because there is no DTB to describe it
#
# Piece 3 is the unverified one. See the patch header before trusting this.

# ── BCM53125 switch: NOW ENABLED ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The kernel side is ea-common/features/switch/linux.fragment and the board glue is
# ea-common/drivers/spi/spi-ea-b53-board.c, both pulled in by the
# 'switch' entry in board/ea3-v2/ohc.features. The glue's old compile failure
# (dsa_chip_data moved out of <net/dsa.h>) is fixed. Port 1's DSA bring-up is
# still unproven on hardware — S40net falls back to the master if DSA does not
# attach, so the board stays reachable through the primary jack regardless.

# FALLBACK, AND ITS LIMIT. Turning CONFIG_NET_DSA off gets you back to plain
# e1000 on eth0, which does reach the network — but only through ONE of the two
# jacks. Measured on a live EA3: with the cable in the primary jack (switch
# port 2) the box is reachable; moved to the other jack (port 1) it goes
# completely dark, while that port's MIB counters still climb (1.4 MB received,
# 4565 multicast frames) and its TxOctets stays at exactly 0.
#
# So port 1 links and receives at the PHY, and the switch simply never forwards
# it to the CPU port. The stock configuration isolates that jack. This is the
# argument FOR the DSA work rather than against it: without a switch driver the
# second RJ45 is unusable, and no amount of e1000 configuration changes that.

# --- kernel command line (EA3) ---
# `nocrs` IS REQUIRED here, same as the EA1 — this was tested both ways.
#
# The ACPI _CRS on this SoC is deficient: the only memory window it declares for
# the root bus is a 48 KB TPM range, and it never mentions the 0xc0000000-
# 0xdfffffff space where every on-SoC device actually lives. Boot WITHOUT nocrs
# and you get:
#
#     pci_bus 0000:00: root bus resource [mem 0xfed40000-0xfed4bfff window]
#     pci 0000:00:01.0: bridge window [mem 0xc0000000-0xdfffffff]:
#                       can't claim; no compatible bridge window
#     ... 70 x "can't claim" ...
#     <hang at t=1.02s, before userspace>
#
# With nocrs the kernel derives windows from E820 and the root bus gets
# [mem 0x00000000-0xffffffff], which covers everything, and the board boots to a
# login prompt.
#
# NOTE on the e1000 -EIO: SOLVED. It was not a host-bridge-window problem. The
# CE5300 GbE has no PHY on the MDIO bus — the MAC is wired back-to-back to the
# BCM53125 — so the ce4100 PHY-address autodetect scanned all 32 addresses,
# found nothing, and e1000_probe() returned a bare -EIO with nothing logged.
# board/ea-common/patches/linux/0003-e1000-ce5300-fake-phy.patch ports Control4's
# "fake PHY" mode from the GPL drop. PROVEN on hardware 2026-08-26 via bootlinux:
#   e1000 0000:01:0c.0: CE5300: no GbE PHY, using fake-PHY mode
#   e1000: eth0 NIC Link is Up 1000 Mbps Full Duplex
# RAM reclaim -- PROVEN ON HARDWARE 2026-08-31.
# MemTotal 190688 kB -> 1807608 kB (1765 MB total, 1693 MB available), a 9.5x
# increase. ACPI clean, zero kernel errors, boots to userspace.
#
# It has to be memmap=exactmap. The additive form does NOT work here and was
# measured failing: memmap=256M@0x10000000 parsed fine (the kernel even printed
# "user-defined physical RAM map") and changed nothing, because e820
# sanitisation resolves overlaps in favour of the HIGHER type -- CEFDK's
# "reserved" (2) beats the RAM (1) being added. exactmap replaces the map
# outright, so there is no overlap to lose.
#
# Every entry below is load-bearing:
#   68K@0        )  low memory, reproduced faithfully
#   60K#0x11000  )  THE ACPI TABLES. Claiming this as RAM (an earlier attempt
#                )  used a blanket memmap=640K@0) gives
#                )     ACPI Error: AE_NO_ACPI_TABLES
#                )  and the box dies just after, because ACPI is what routes
#                )  the interrupts. It boots to 706 MB and then hangs.
#   512K@0x20000 )  rest of low memory
#   255M@1M      )  the region CEFDK gave us (0x100000-0xc7fffff) MERGED with
#                )  the 0x0c800000-0x0fffffff gap above it -- contiguous to 256M
#   1536M@0x10000000 ) the reclaim, out of the 1.8 GB media carve-out. Ends at
#                )  0x70000000, leaving 128 MB of margin before the 0x78000000
#                )  stop line and far below the framebuffer at 0x7fc00000.
#
# Do NOT widen past 0x78000000. The framebuffer must not become System RAM or
# ce5300-fb refuses to probe and the display goes; and the region just under it
# is where CEFDK keeps structures the box needs in order to reboot.
#
# Note this crosses the 32-bit lowmem boundary: the kernel reports ~934 MB of
# highmem. Userspace is unaffected; kernel allocations stay in lowmem.
#
# EA-3 v2 ONLY. The 199M@1M figure describes THIS board's map; a board whose
# CEFDK hands out a different split needs its own numbers, and a memmap past
# the end of real DRAM is unrecoverable remotely.
CONFIG_CMDLINE="console=ttyS0,115200 earlyprintk=serial,ttyS0,115200 pci=realloc,nocrs memmap=exactmap memmap=68K@0 memmap=60K#0x11000 memmap=512K@0x20000 memmap=255M@1M memmap=1536M@0x10000000"

# --- RAM: CEFDK hides 90% of it -------------------------------------------
#
# MEASURED on hardware 2026-08-28. The board has 2 GB of DDR3 fitted and Linux
# boots with MemTotal = 190720 kB. The missing memory is not missing, it is
# withheld: CEFDK's e820 declares
#
#   [mem 0x00100000-0x0c7fffff]  System RAM       <- 200 MB, all Linux gets
#   [mem 0x0c800000-0x7fffffff]  device reserved  <- 1.8 GB, carved out
#
# That carve-out is for the Intel CE media stack -- 1080p decode buffers, demux
# rings, display planes -- which openHC does not load and will never load. The
# reference design for this SoC is a set-top box; we are not one.
#
# Evidence the memory is real and idle, not merely address space:
#   - /proc/iomem shows exactly one claim in the whole 1.8 GB: our own
#     framebuffer, ce5300-fb at 0x7fc00000. simplefb draws through it and the
#     panel shows the picture, so DRAM at 2 GB minus 4 MB demonstrably works.
#   - A word at 0x40000000 (1 GB) took 0xa5a5a5a5 and 0x5a5a5a5a and read each
#     back, then restored; the neighbouring word was undisturbed, so the
#     decode is real and nothing is aliasing down into low memory.
#   - Much of the range still holds a boot-time address-walking test pattern
#     (0x30000000 reads 0x30000000), i.e. nothing has written it since CEFDK.
#
# This matters because it decides what can run here. WPE WebKit does not fit in
# 186 MB; it fits comfortably in 1.2 GB. See https://the1truejoe.github.io/openHC/ea/graphics/.
#
# memmap=nn@ss forces a range back to E820 System RAM, and it is additive --
# without "exactmap" the rest of the map is left alone. The line below claims
# 1 GB starting at 256 MB, deliberately stopping well short of the framebuffer
# at 0x7fc00000:
#
#CONFIG_CMDLINE="console=ttyS0,115200 earlyprintk=serial,ttyS0,115200 pci=realloc,nocrs memmap=1024M@0x10000000"
#
# HARD LIMIT: the range must never reach the framebuffer at 0x7fc00000.
# ce5300-fb refuses to probe if its buffer has become System RAM --
#
#     if (page_is_ram(PFN_DOWN(fb_base))) {
#             pr_err("ce5300-fb: 0x%lx is System RAM, refusing\n", fb_base);
#
# -- so a memmap= that swallows it costs you the display, not just the memory.
# It fails loudly with that exact line in dmesg, which is the good case; the
# bad case is widening far enough to also take the CEFDK structures the box
# needs to reboot. 1024M@0x10000000 ends at 0x50000000, well clear.
#
# SUPERSEDED: the additive memmap= line below does not work on this hardware --
# see the CONFIG_CMDLINE above for what does, and why. Kept only as a record of
# what was tried.
#
# It should be turned on with the serial console in front
# of you. If the reclaimed range turns out to be live after all, the kernel
# does not warn -- it corrupts and panics, and this board has no remote
# recovery: getting back in needs the factory-restore button and the CEFDK
# shell. Bring it up in stages (256M, then 1024M, then out to 0x78000000, the
# last address before the uninitialised region under the framebuffer) and run
# something memory-heavy at each step before widening.
#
# EA-3 v2 only. Do not promote this to ea-common: the EA-1 boards have not been
# measured and a memmap= past the end of real DRAM is unrecoverable.

# eMMC storage (sdhci-pci + ext4) comes from ea-common/features/emmc/linux.fragment
# via the 'emmc' entry in board/ea3-v2/ohc.features.
